; ======================================================================
;  hw_board.pi3 - the Raspberry Pi 3's HwBoard* backend.
;
;  The handful of facts the payload ABI (Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi) needs and no
;  command family does: what this board is called, how to reset it, what
;  state its MMU and caches are in, what time it is, and how to pet the
;  deadman. None of them has a #CAP_ flag for the same reason HwAddr* has
;  none - every board has an identity, a reset and a cache state, so
;  there is nothing that could be absent.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi3/Lib/timer.pbi (Pi3Micros,
;  Pi3DelayUs), RaspberryPi3/Lib/mmu.pi3 (MmuState) and
;  RaspberryPi3/Lib/pl011_console.pi3 (UartDrain), and before
;  Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi, which calls all of them. House rule 10: this file
;  includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i HwBoardName()
  ProcedureReturn "Raspberry Pi 3 Model B (Broadcom BCM2837)"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwReboot() - reset the board. Does not return.
;
;  THE BCM2835 POWER-MANAGEMENT WATCHDOG, which is how everything resets
;  a Pi: write a short timeout into WDOG and then set the full-reset bit
;  in RSTC. The magic $5A in the top byte is the PM block's password, and
;  a write without it is discarded silently.
;
;  THE SAME TWO REGISTERS RaspberryPi3/Lib/update_watchdog.pbi WRITES,
;  and the values are taken from there rather than re-derived. This is a
;  second copy of two register addresses and it is deliberate: that
;  file's Pi3UpdateResetNow() refuses unless the updater's transport is
;  quiescent, which is exactly right for a reset requested in the middle
;  of a firmware update and exactly wrong for `reboot` typed at a prompt.
;  A monitor whose reset command could refuse because something unrelated
;  was mid-transaction is a monitor somebody power-cycles instead.
;
;  THE CONSOLE IS DRAINED FIRST. BUSY, not FIFO-empty - UartDrain() waits
;  for the last stop bit to leave the wire. The difference is one
;  character time, and it is the difference between a clean last line and
;  a truncated one.
;
;  THE DELAY AFTERWARDS IS NOT A WAIT, IT IS THE PROOF. The reset takes a
;  handful of ticks; reaching the line after the delay means the reset
;  did NOT happen, and saying so is better than returning quietly to a
;  prompt the operator will think belongs to the new boot.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwReboot()
  Define control.i
  UartDrain()
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
  ; WDOG = password | 10 ticks of the 65536 Hz counter.
  PokeL($3F100024, $5A00000A)
  control = PeekL($3F10001C) & $FFFFFFFF
  ; RSTC: keep the bits the firmware set, clear the two-bit reset-config
  ; field and write "full reset" into it.
  PokeL($3F10001C, $5A000000 | (control & $00FFFFCF) | $20)
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
  Pi3DelayUs(500000)
  PrintN("!! the reset did not happen. The power-management watchdog accepted the")
  PrintN("   write and the board is still here, which usually means the firmware")
  PrintN("   holds the reset configuration. Remove the power.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pi3EnterPayload(a) - call the address, and come back.
;
;  A BLR AND NOT A BR. The payload is CALLED, so a plain `ret` at its end
;  brings the processor back here with this monitor still resident and
;  still owning the card, the console and the page tables. A branch would
;  be a handover, and this board already has one of those - the loader's,
;  which is how this image was started.
;
;  x0 IS THE ENTRY POINT AND x0 IS ALSO THE FIRST ARGUMENT, which is
;  convenient and worth stating rather than discovering: a payload built
;  from this tree takes no arguments, so what it actually receives in x0
;  is its own address, and nothing reads it.
;
;  THE BARRIERS ARE THE POINT. The payload's bytes were written as DATA,
;  with the caches on; without the instruction-cache invalidate the fetch
;  at its entry can see whatever was there before, which is the failure
;  that produces no message at all. Anvil/Core/memcmd.pbi has already
;  cleaned the range by address through CacheFlushRange(); this is the
;  fence that makes the clean visible to the fetch.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Pi3EnterPayload(a.i)
  ASM
    dsb sy
    ic iallu
    dsb sy
    isb
    blr x0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pi3MonWatchdogStop() - legacy A/B recovery helper, not normal kernel boot.
;
;  THE MOST IMPORTANT WRITE THIS MONITOR MAKES. The immutable loader arms
;  a fifteen-second never-fed watchdog before it branches here and does
;  not stop it; this is what turns it off, once the running slot has
;  confirmed itself. Miss it and the board resets and falls back to the
;  last confirmed image, which from the other end of a serial cable looks
;  exactly like this image having crashed.
;
;  $5A000102 IS THE SAME WORD RaspberryPi3/Lib/update_watchdog.pbi WRITES
;  and it is taken from there rather than re-derived: the PM password in
;  the top byte, the reset-config field cleared, and the watchdog-enable
;  bit off. This is a second copy of two register addresses and it is
;  deliberate, for the same reason HwReboot() above is: that file's
;  Pi3UpdateWatchdogStop() refuses unless the A/B module has a confirmed
;  active slot IN ITS OWN GLOBALS, and this monitor confirms through
;  Anvil/Storage/ab_record.pbi instead - by name through the shared
;  filesystem rather than by walking a cluster chain, which is what rule
;  30 requires of it. Coupling the release of the deadman to the state of
;  a module this monitor deliberately does not use would be a monitor
;  that cannot switch its own watchdog off.
;
;  IT VERIFIES. Reading the control register back and checking the enable
;  bits are clear costs one load, and the alternative is a monitor that
;  believes it is safe and is fifteen seconds from a reset.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Pi3MonWatchdogStop()
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
  PokeL($3F10001C, $5A000102)
  ASM
    dsb sy
    isb
  EndASM
  ProcedureReturn Bool((PeekL($3F10001C) & $30) = 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmuState() - bit 0 MMU on, bit 1 D-cache on, bit 2 I-cache on.
;
;  READ FROM SCTLR, not answered from a constant, because on this board
;  Anvil is the thing that turns them on: it is entered with all three
;  off and brings them up itself, so the register is the only honest
;  source. A payload that asked this and got a constant would be told the
;  cache state of an intention rather than of the machine.
;
;  MmuState() reads the register of the CURRENT exception level, which
;  matters here because this monitor can arrive at EL2 or at EL3
;  depending on what the firmware was asked to do.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmuState()
  ProcedureReturn MmuState()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMicros() IS NOT HERE. It is part of THE TIME seam
;  (Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi) and this board implements that whole seam in one
;  file, RaspberryPi3/Board/hwtimer.pi3, so that a payload and the
;  monitor cannot end up reading the counter two different ways.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwPet() - pet the deadman.
;
;  A NO-OP, AND AN HONEST ONE RATHER THAN A MISSING FEATURE. There is a
;  watchdog on this part and this monitor deliberately does not hold it:
;  the immutable loader arms a fifteen-second never-fed timer before it
;  branches here, and this monitor's job is to RELEASE it by confirming
;  the slot, not to start feeding it. A monitor that petted would defeat
;  the A/B trial outright - a slot that hung after confirming would be
;  petted for ever instead of falling back to the last good one.
;
;  A payload that pets in its own loop therefore costs one call and
;  nothing happens, which is the correct behaviour: the alternative is a
;  payload that has to know which board it is on in order to know whether
;  to pet, and that is the fork the whole capability model exists to
;  prevent. When this board grows a `deadman` command of its own, this
;  becomes the feed and nothing else in the tree changes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwPet()
EndProcedure
